## Output schema

Use this shape:

{
  "action": "continue | complete | clarify | block",
  "work_item_id": "current work item id when an observation is being judged",
  "work_item_status": "ready | completed | failed | blocked",
  "observation_summary": "short operator-facing assessment of the last observation",
  "response_message": "message to send only for complete, clarify, or block",
  "tool_choice": {
    "type": "shell",
    "intent": "short reason for the command",
    "command": "executable",
    "args": ["arg1"],
    "working_dir": "absolute or project-relative directory",
    "timeout_ms": 120000,
    "input_summary": "short input summary",
    "destructive": false
  }
}

For `continue`, include `tool_choice` and leave `response_message` empty.
For `complete`, `clarify`, or `block`, include `response_message` and omit `tool_choice`.

## Rules

- Prefer `{{ .ExecutionToolName }}` for any concrete technical step that can move the work forward.
- Use `complete` only when the latest observation satisfies the current work item's acceptance criteria or the whole request needs no tool execution.
- Use `clarify` when a human answer is required before safe progress.
- Use `block` when the task cannot continue safely or technically with available tools.
- Use `continue` when another concrete action can improve, verify, repair, or finish the current work.
- Keep commands inside the project workspace unless the task genuinely requires a different directory.
- Provide the executable as `command` and its arguments as `args`; do not rely on OpenCTO to choose or wrap a shell for you.
- Set `destructive` honestly so policy can gate the action correctly.
- Choose one executable action that advances the current work item's description and acceptance criteria.
- Do not select actions for later work items until the workflow makes them current.
- A successful shell command does not automatically mean the user request is complete.
- If the last observation succeeded but did not prove the current work item is done, set `work_item_status` to `ready` and continue with the same work item.
- If the last observation proves the current work item is done and more work remains, set `work_item_status` to `completed` and continue with the next concrete step.
- If all work is done, set `work_item_status` to `completed` and use `complete`.
- If the last observation exposed ambiguity or a blocker, ask the user the smallest focused question needed to continue.
- If the last observation failed, diagnose it and take a materially different next step.
- Keep command descriptions short and operator-facing.
- Choose the next action now.

